  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 217 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: \n\nHigh clouds are in the process of increasing from west-to-east \nacross northern New York and Vermont this afternoon, associated \nwith mid-level trough extending from northern Ontario southward \nacross Lake Huron, Lake Erie and into Ohio. PBL winds have \nturned southerly with 1022mb sfc anticyclone now east of Cape \nCod. Southerly winds should remain moderate overnight with \napproaching frontal system and absence of much radiational \ncooling overnight. Overnight lows will generally range from the \nlow-mid 60s. With the increasing southerly gradient flow, a Lake\nWind Advisory has been posted (see Marine section below for \ntonight. The Lake Wind Advisory may be necessary through the \nmorning hours on Monday as well. \n\nNot much in the way of mid-tropospheric height falls overnight,\nbut some low-level WAA in advance of the approaching front \nshould be just enough to yield scattered rain showers overnight,\nespecially after 03Z or so. There is some marginal elevated \nCAPE for parcels lifted from 850-775mb per HRRR and NAM3 \nforecast soundings. As such, kept with the idea that there could\nbe a few embedded convective elements with a few overnight \nrumbles of thunder possible. PoPs are highest across central/nrn\nVT during the pre-dawn hours (70-80%) and generally 30-50% \nelsewhere. \n\nHeading into Monday...surface boundary progresses through \nslowly from west to east. If we get some breaks in the cloud \ncover, there is a chance for modest PBL destabilization by late \nmorning/early afternoon. Consistent with this, the 12Z HRRR \ndeveloped 500-800 J/kg SBCAPE during the peak heating hours \nMonday, that is relatively uncapped. Anticipating a separate \nbroken line of showers - and perhaps a few embedded \nthunderstorms - to develop during the midday hours while \ndrifting slowly ewd across central/ern VT by mid-late afternoon.\nWeak mid-level lapse rates and modest sfc-6km shear suggest \nthat if thunderstorms form, they are unlikely to become severe. \nHowever, have shown a separate period of 60-70% PoPs to account \nfor this expected separate period of convective showers and \nstorms during Monday afternoon. High temperatures on Monday will\nbe seasonable to slightly below average for mid-August, in the \nmid- upper 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Lingering showers will move out on Wednesday \nnight and Thursday as broad cyclonic flow slowly migrates \neastward. After the system moves off, a period of drier weather \nlooks to return for the latter half of the week. Daytime \ntemperatures increase towards climatological normals into next \nweekend with highs in the 70s and lower 80s as well as overnight\nlows falling into the 40s and 50s. There is the potential for \nanother system to sweep through next weekend bringing some \nsustained showers. We'll continue to monitor this potential as \nthe event approaches.",
